<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Écija</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Astigi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Écija is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the Genil river. As the Roman colony of Astigi it was an important centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> production, and it was a bishopric under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>. Under Muslim rule it was a town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> disputed in the revolts against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> took it in 1240.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecija.jpg" alt="Écija" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Рустам Абдрахимов · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parroquia_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
